[{:db/id "q-332714/selection/d43a186e7f693e6ca828794097ba60df3d8545f92ed4fee2f13c5de3e16161ac" :answer/id #uuid "d5c9fc18-bd37-5e6e-8220-9dcd5ab4cbd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332714/selection/d128089a44cca1805db5e6fcf0cd7cd0a615c376029820c8610c2ab3f494803f" :answer/id #uuid "552c54bb-941b-5e17-b41e-1693be0ff973" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-9x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332714/selection/f649fa2a2650ba9e3005726af53cfea3d360aca6f868f4b90808da7bb31891bc" :answer/id #uuid "24465602-7f1b-539f-aa24-b464ebfe7916" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-18x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332714/selection/2afcf90cede13b422db5b3cae7cad23e5f8ad73933374a8ce19219553c69df4f" :answer/id #uuid "54095541-0443-5cb3-bb1a-87635bc7783a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{9x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332714/selection/0f1a8f5ee824e72e421877c7835bdd238a4d9bf5edca53865c984fad92ccc854" :answer/id #uuid "d539de16-5135-5bb9-a8c2-da4a0ba70dea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{18x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332714/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "482e4922-b9f8-5421-a3d1-5a087d4d4c3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332714/selection/d128089a44cca1805db5e6fcf0cd7cd0a615c376029820c8610c2ab3f494803f" :answer-field/choices ["q-332714/selection/d43a186e7f693e6ca828794097ba60df3d8545f92ed4fee2f13c5de3e16161ac" "q-332714/selection/d128089a44cca1805db5e6fcf0cd7cd0a615c376029820c8610c2ab3f494803f" "q-332714/selection/f649fa2a2650ba9e3005726af53cfea3d360aca6f868f4b90808da7bb31891bc" "q-332714/selection/2afcf90cede13b422db5b3cae7cad23e5f8ad73933374a8ce19219553c69df4f" "q-332714/selection/0f1a8f5ee824e72e421877c7835bdd238a4d9bf5edca53865c984fad92ccc854"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332714" :question/id #uuid "5a19b74b-a106-5c2d-8c3b-4cbd7c24e4ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-332714" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+9y=6x-5.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-9{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-9\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -9\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -9\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-9x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-9x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-9x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-9x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-9x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332714/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332714"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332741/selection/8281fb2c6143edb145490a468776d771f5f9f90e1ca18c46667de351f5a06b58" :answer/id #uuid "1c3a9c0d-c11d-5c9b-803c-d825e79a7653" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-16x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332741/selection/25190161a14374119a91017fdaa1ec58a6964eeca6c19d4008b1593387376f00" :answer/id #uuid "bba209e6-6d48-5d9b-9849-d599bcba0162" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{16x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332741/selection/2a63dfcc87c5a8d49816fb12c6f35925a87c6ed4e45646902e2043620aa1f905" :answer/id #uuid "a336320c-3c6b-5594-aaac-be768d9a90a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{8x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332741/selection/ff178e0cc2a2b7b20ff2eca53c6bed5ba0f812685f7779924e74da8674d27c4b" :answer/id #uuid "25cf3dde-55a0-53d3-ab9b-9242b4255768" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-8x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332741/selection/34a4d69876ce99c36316c67d5fc2285e237034c7e479edd3662f9dc5b6a99147" :answer/id #uuid "55771132-3496-5aac-92a1-81aef0eba17d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332741/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "669dee12-8c5e-57aa-9a9a-d0ebc0e335ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332741/selection/ff178e0cc2a2b7b20ff2eca53c6bed5ba0f812685f7779924e74da8674d27c4b" :answer-field/choices ["q-332741/selection/8281fb2c6143edb145490a468776d771f5f9f90e1ca18c46667de351f5a06b58" "q-332741/selection/25190161a14374119a91017fdaa1ec58a6964eeca6c19d4008b1593387376f00" "q-332741/selection/2a63dfcc87c5a8d49816fb12c6f35925a87c6ed4e45646902e2043620aa1f905" "q-332741/selection/ff178e0cc2a2b7b20ff2eca53c6bed5ba0f812685f7779924e74da8674d27c4b" "q-332741/selection/34a4d69876ce99c36316c67d5fc2285e237034c7e479edd3662f9dc5b6a99147"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332741" :question/id #uuid "a34f15af-0935-50c9-ba42-ca8012158411" :question/math-academy-id "q-332741" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+8y=-13.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+8{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-8{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-8\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -8\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -8\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-8x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-8x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-8x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-8x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-8x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332741/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332741"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-332701"] :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7y=3x+4.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-7{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-7\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -7\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -7\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-7x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-7x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-7x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-7x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-331877/selection/c61fc87e5e5c9c858605a734a5d8aeafb2f428c5f1d1a2ec380b791d66bbbdcd" :answer/id #uuid "1c58247e-7981-56da-8b5e-47ae4ce2630a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331877/selection/bee8c6ba40c00c089738a3825830a5665740093ef771fbbfac5dd805221ece2e" :answer/id #uuid "be564220-7731-5480-b9e5-fa73ceef3eb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331877/selection/94caadd94e8f8f0f5a72ef8707b1508aca287e906c9df3e5318abda56e430702" :answer/id #uuid "73770f48-2dfb-5828-95b6-c6f72b8882dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331877/selection/39c004a25915781a6897602005bd3c868d4057e2925463856ceb775d935cf10b" :answer/id #uuid "2015e534-fba6-51c1-a9d5-9ae2b3892120" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331877/selection/69f13eed5a850a91183ca0c0d99a4d4f3a4f36280da69f70b7b7b40321574467" :answer/id #uuid "ce55c6ac-cee0-5916-b7ad-2ef46a12a4de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331877/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a954b816-d048-57b5-afe1-dd002c9cd01f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-331877/selection/39c004a25915781a6897602005bd3c868d4057e2925463856ceb775d935cf10b" :answer-field/choices ["q-331877/selection/c61fc87e5e5c9c858605a734a5d8aeafb2f428c5f1d1a2ec380b791d66bbbdcd" "q-331877/selection/bee8c6ba40c00c089738a3825830a5665740093ef771fbbfac5dd805221ece2e" "q-331877/selection/94caadd94e8f8f0f5a72ef8707b1508aca287e906c9df3e5318abda56e430702" "q-331877/selection/39c004a25915781a6897602005bd3c868d4057e2925463856ceb775d935cf10b" "q-331877/selection/69f13eed5a850a91183ca0c0d99a4d4f3a4f36280da69f70b7b7b40321574467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331877" :question/id #uuid "279c6e70-99fa-5736-91b3-186d8e98a23c" :question/math-academy-id "q-331877" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=3{x}^{2}-2x+5.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{4x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-331877/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331877"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332704/selection/ff178e0cc2a2b7b20ff2eca53c6bed5ba0f812685f7779924e74da8674d27c4b" :answer/id #uuid "7e925c98-38b1-58d1-83f6-92c12bb9469d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-8x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332704/selection/bee8c6ba40c00c089738a3825830a5665740093ef771fbbfac5dd805221ece2e" :answer/id #uuid "a85d9de8-36cf-5344-bd94-8bd4c73813e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332704/selection/2a63dfcc87c5a8d49816fb12c6f35925a87c6ed4e45646902e2043620aa1f905" :answer/id #uuid "1a2bf25c-f2b3-5734-b888-15d2f3e113a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{8x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332704/selection/1d58f4ad044acb6156300b92774f2ab5c577de50c5c44d4ace0202cf215279cf" :answer/id #uuid "b02d7633-1a0c-54cb-beff-2b6707e1f6de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332704/selection/39c004a25915781a6897602005bd3c868d4057e2925463856ceb775d935cf10b" :answer/id #uuid "80af1a97-c5fb-55e9-85ee-cf2f7163a877" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332704/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "66611d77-d5e4-54bb-935b-01e52e22c116" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332704/selection/39c004a25915781a6897602005bd3c868d4057e2925463856ceb775d935cf10b" :answer-field/choices ["q-332704/selection/ff178e0cc2a2b7b20ff2eca53c6bed5ba0f812685f7779924e74da8674d27c4b" "q-332704/selection/bee8c6ba40c00c089738a3825830a5665740093ef771fbbfac5dd805221ece2e" "q-332704/selection/2a63dfcc87c5a8d49816fb12c6f35925a87c6ed4e45646902e2043620aa1f905" "q-332704/selection/1d58f4ad044acb6156300b92774f2ab5c577de50c5c44d4ace0202cf215279cf" "q-332704/selection/39c004a25915781a6897602005bd3c868d4057e2925463856ceb775d935cf10b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332704" :question/id #uuid "1b716f9d-06f2-5b91-b42d-dc103f036078" :question/math-academy-id "q-332704" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-x+6.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{4x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332704/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332704"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332295/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "a02a2559-6128-5c31-8b49-279e748d4e47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332295/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "805fa3fb-59af-5323-aae2-cfa62895ebf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332295/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "37346fd5-4175-5566-9ae4-0e53ec30c5df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332295/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-332295/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-332295/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332295" :question/id #uuid "6e254e74-aca3-5cd2-a47c-6d3276e604d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332295" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=-20.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha =-20\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332295/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332295"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-332265"] :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=12.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =12\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-332302/field-1/186e415469c1cda52c815710b2cebac0467cf7fe4ab401af767ee19afe159e8e" :answer/id #uuid "ef49e4d0-03df-5674-8144-b982a7a45316" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332302/field-1/a5cfc473b620ae5c123c09dcaa728fbaaee259b3e4e1b09c71a03b9f84e7aa11" :answer/id #uuid "2e7d4a37-ff77-5e7c-8b1e-63a23a918bd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332302/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ed3ae9e1-2798-53dc-a38f-5465ec956d54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332302/field-1/186e415469c1cda52c815710b2cebac0467cf7fe4ab401af767ee19afe159e8e" :answer-field/choices ["q-332302/field-1/186e415469c1cda52c815710b2cebac0467cf7fe4ab401af767ee19afe159e8e" "q-332302/field-1/a5cfc473b620ae5c123c09dcaa728fbaaee259b3e4e1b09c71a03b9f84e7aa11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332302" :question/id #uuid "069198d4-e282-5b46-ba93-6cbba62d1687" :question/math-academy-id "q-332302" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=8x+9.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +8(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x+9\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n8\\alpha x+(\\alpha +8\\beta )=8x+9.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +8\\beta =9\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x+1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332302/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332302"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332289/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "fa703ac5-040e-530e-8fc6-2d203511a822" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332289/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "38b7a90c-bb3e-54d0-ba39-8414e6256c52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332289/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-332289/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332289" :question/id #uuid "b28d5fe7-c6cd-5018-9178-d5a601b91e1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-332289" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=15.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\alpha =15\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332289/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332289"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332312/selection/7d963c7d9d2752c30c22ea3136c0f3dfcbf75a16fcc9a21e393ac90eb98749e1" :answer/id #uuid "62f261c2-cd14-58c0-80df-ddeb79a72f2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332312/selection/c1f2db5dc23555a6c31fa2dc5156d23aa54a1cce5a49c20652b0bc6b4aac682f" :answer/id #uuid "c0f28f54-c8e7-5d9d-9244-9b01ed1a03d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+2x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332312/selection/d6de2a1db91e5be469362efd255854452faf165e5cc8db536b1913b08cb89516" :answer/id #uuid "da18f759-ff18-5864-9fc5-f37402153961" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332312/selection/8d1bfd8d5b5ba0816b74729ede616a4cff22ffa858f47ffa37b0f7e486367cc4" :answer/id #uuid "31a0a489-a3c9-5a77-8220-5ef43af0f206" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3{x}^{2}+2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332312/selection/1b862d1fe112b8a755a5acd8b33f1b0168653669fe0c46a1c9ed1da48ae9e3cd" :answer/id #uuid "af4129b6-f61c-5770-bf82-d79508231cf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332312/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ef720cc0-1e9e-5d72-af64-8ca46565f4fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332312/selection/1b862d1fe112b8a755a5acd8b33f1b0168653669fe0c46a1c9ed1da48ae9e3cd" :answer-field/choices ["q-332312/selection/7d963c7d9d2752c30c22ea3136c0f3dfcbf75a16fcc9a21e393ac90eb98749e1" "q-332312/selection/c1f2db5dc23555a6c31fa2dc5156d23aa54a1cce5a49c20652b0bc6b4aac682f" "q-332312/selection/d6de2a1db91e5be469362efd255854452faf165e5cc8db536b1913b08cb89516" "q-332312/selection/8d1bfd8d5b5ba0816b74729ede616a4cff22ffa858f47ffa37b0f7e486367cc4" "q-332312/selection/1b862d1fe112b8a755a5acd8b33f1b0168653669fe0c46a1c9ed1da48ae9e3cd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332312" :question/id #uuid "0059abd6-dc4c-559c-9481-34b1f39c6f24" :question/math-academy-id "q-332312" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-332312/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332312"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332556/selection/99df5ec311fbb13dc03c39e0731594e1e75ff56ced57f495ae55dd44a4869e15" :answer/id #uuid "7ece337a-30e2-5059-a6a1-fdab3768f67b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+2{x}^{2}-4x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332556/selection/d602bed34bc8cbf2d1be3eb6ae776d8a35501a3c63f514db1bd3967121f5b0fa" :answer/id #uuid "1318eef8-93f7-570e-aa25-456973883908" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-2x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332556/selection/8ff493a06262158636f3fa38b13f162403813ebee445b7eef9d0ea19be4ac8db" :answer/id #uuid "8195fd33-4ab7-5e3b-84f6-7ede3d063976" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-{x}^{2}-4x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332556/selection/5a1b697849a3646b8e2913b48ca17213eca74ef7c0a5068f6edfebdc9c7b946b" :answer/id #uuid "75e06894-732c-54e4-b86f-e8d71ccb76ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-4x-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332556/selection/407a116951014f99909f0ed72e095eadecb8b7c0a73a6fdc403f593e03431cc8" :answer/id #uuid "3c9118bc-ed65-5ad8-905d-c0def882717f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-4x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332556/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "595bef39-61a0-5f5b-9dd1-0332bad33c71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332556/selection/407a116951014f99909f0ed72e095eadecb8b7c0a73a6fdc403f593e03431cc8" :answer-field/choices ["q-332556/selection/99df5ec311fbb13dc03c39e0731594e1e75ff56ced57f495ae55dd44a4869e15" "q-332556/selection/d602bed34bc8cbf2d1be3eb6ae776d8a35501a3c63f514db1bd3967121f5b0fa" "q-332556/selection/8ff493a06262158636f3fa38b13f162403813ebee445b7eef9d0ea19be4ac8db" "q-332556/selection/5a1b697849a3646b8e2913b48ca17213eca74ef7c0a5068f6edfebdc9c7b946b" "q-332556/selection/407a116951014f99909f0ed72e095eadecb8b7c0a73a6fdc403f593e03431cc8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332556" :question/id #uuid "ce3e0989-e7de-5d8b-bc2e-aea033858039" :question/math-academy-id "q-332556" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=-{x}^{2}+6x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$  we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+6x-7\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -\\beta )x+(\\beta -\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+6x-7\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta -\\gamma =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =-4,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}-4x+3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-4x+3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-332556/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332556"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332376/field-1/e8dd1962901ca12de798295db53b0890a95e73c81effc57ac2c411652df7c1a7" :answer/id #uuid "b4fb5d74-8054-59d8-a0aa-af0380bf1c10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{3x}-3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332376/field-1/39f6776fbf6c106657a3e6c697c851a2a75a0c0fb259615245bebb1ffb2652dd" :answer/id #uuid "a145f527-5c8e-5e9c-8126-676a9617c31d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{3x}+3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332376/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "44504acb-40f3-5cc4-ad0e-151cd18d2e33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332376/field-1/e8dd1962901ca12de798295db53b0890a95e73c81effc57ac2c411652df7c1a7" :answer-field/choices ["q-332376/field-1/e8dd1962901ca12de798295db53b0890a95e73c81effc57ac2c411652df7c1a7" "q-332376/field-1/39f6776fbf6c106657a3e6c697c851a2a75a0c0fb259615245bebb1ffb2652dd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332376" :question/id #uuid "fc235b3a-1731-5566-a624-628471650abf" :question/math-academy-id "q-332376" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=9x-3.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -3(\\alpha x+\\beta )=9x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\alpha x+(\\alpha -3\\beta )=9x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha =9\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -3\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-3x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332376/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332376"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-332543"] :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=6.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+3\\beta =6\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-331920/selection/a16139964fd9a955cacb202867ae679602ecdabf641b28642d6c89d3a79e67cd" :answer/id #uuid "0f874757-e58d-5f3a-969b-2de0aa96f4b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-x+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331920/selection/1e83103440c80236ce4e9e0ca50a70c9134ecbcf6d576fd88894457003a3f7ba" :answer/id #uuid "55ab4aa1-0dbe-52d4-8331-29491c0009fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331920/selection/9032d0b5ae8a832bd24da33099d1a3abb116146b9760976e9c9b4ea5ded1a158" :answer/id #uuid "669440ea-5538-5ea8-99c9-0fb8326df3e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-4x+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331920/selection/1f45de964df38def77ddda8c0c14d89e95f454b4ef4ac9181c7991e759e12330" :answer/id #uuid "b3234d59-b988-5b25-98fc-55cbdac73e55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-4x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331920/selection/9cb75afdf731101a679a3ed36639b62864d87003155a961b72425a86a66b8820" :answer/id #uuid "72541d41-5ad8-522a-a4ab-b75179903f0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331920/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec8a9c39-5604-5b2d-b090-9fee7d8b9984" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-331920/selection/1e83103440c80236ce4e9e0ca50a70c9134ecbcf6d576fd88894457003a3f7ba" :answer-field/choices ["q-331920/selection/a16139964fd9a955cacb202867ae679602ecdabf641b28642d6c89d3a79e67cd" "q-331920/selection/1e83103440c80236ce4e9e0ca50a70c9134ecbcf6d576fd88894457003a3f7ba" "q-331920/selection/9032d0b5ae8a832bd24da33099d1a3abb116146b9760976e9c9b4ea5ded1a158" "q-331920/selection/1f45de964df38def77ddda8c0c14d89e95f454b4ef4ac9181c7991e759e12330" "q-331920/selection/9cb75afdf731101a679a3ed36639b62864d87003155a961b72425a86a66b8820"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331920" :question/id #uuid "d9cadaa1-0348-5108-b0ed-d95da25f0147" :question/math-academy-id "q-331920" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=4{x}^{2}-4x+1.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\beta -2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha -2\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta -2\\gamma =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =-\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-331920/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331920"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332547/field-1/452c47bc5ef895424a6ff0311915822b3c562b5cc13fc7de5496f8f329c442df" :answer/id #uuid "4b7489ad-303a-579f-8d97-f60645cc3f97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{-6x}-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332547/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6e0f49b-23d4-525a-8baa-9e59b924e623" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332547/field-1/452c47bc5ef895424a6ff0311915822b3c562b5cc13fc7de5496f8f329c442df" :answer-field/choices ["q-332547/field-1/452c47bc5ef895424a6ff0311915822b3c562b5cc13fc7de5496f8f329c442df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332547" :question/id #uuid "0437cac1-0430-51b8-a73f-f9f901b83c52" :question/math-academy-id "q-332547" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-30.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 0+6\\beta =-30\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-6x}-5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332547/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332547"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333057/field-1/a1959920ac229e6ef8fe3c0d26e23f7f07701f0188f4f14915e9f960510702c9" :answer/id #uuid "00adc0be-f7a7-5401-ad04-a5424f105846" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7e^{2x}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333057/field-1/374ba76bfbec4d06908d02b072dbfe77dbc956fc305ff10681cf10142b8b3e55" :answer/id #uuid "cc5497ea-33d0-5f4b-877d-4acdeac8c443" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7e^{2x}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333057/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5ec826e-d1b6-518c-8292-d0f81ca1b2e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333057/field-1/a1959920ac229e6ef8fe3c0d26e23f7f07701f0188f4f14915e9f960510702c9" :answer-field/choices ["q-333057/field-1/a1959920ac229e6ef8fe3c0d26e23f7f07701f0188f4f14915e9f960510702c9" "q-333057/field-1/374ba76bfbec4d06908d02b072dbfe77dbc956fc305ff10681cf10142b8b3e55"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333057" :question/id #uuid "693b99ff-648b-58e8-8cc1-0950d6dd7372" :question/math-academy-id "q-333057" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=4,\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a constant, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-2\\beta =4\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\beta $ gives $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=5$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}-2 & =5 \\\\ A-2 & =5 \\\\ A & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=7{e}^{2x}-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333057/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333057"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333052/field-1/bb07facee4bb7c554344edadcb1e9ffd2968e38fbbf89d4f9aae900387e01440" :answer/id #uuid "05ef04e4-5980-59f8-b00a-478ee63bb877" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12e^{x}-2x-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333052/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d87bf10-e58d-506c-960b-9ab113128e61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333052/field-1/bb07facee4bb7c554344edadcb1e9ffd2968e38fbbf89d4f9aae900387e01440" :answer-field/choices ["q-333052/field-1/bb07facee4bb7c554344edadcb1e9ffd2968e38fbbf89d4f9aae900387e01440"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333052" :question/id #uuid "07a5e239-db71-5a83-b584-0e6f1063a68a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333052" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2x+7,\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2x+7\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -(\\alpha x+\\beta )=2x+7\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\alpha x+(\\alpha -\\beta )=2x+7\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -\\beta =7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =-9.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x-9.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}-2x-9.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}-2(0)-9 & =3 \\\\ A-9 & =3 \\\\ A & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=12\\,{e}^{x}-2x-9.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333052/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333052"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333045/selection/a3ea53be90e0ad0edd6de1b4c58b9c898c6d85d84d10a4eda4167c50ef09155c" :answer/id #uuid "a92e6602-1191-5eac-9b37-fe6411e0efa4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333045/selection/1f298ed05360d8e0a100b7fe5415ba6d5d8b7db89366bd588d0a3c7df554fba0" :answer/id #uuid "c4f14d54-3b68-5511-8ebc-94d7e7ef9dac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-9{e}^{-2x}-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333045/selection/3e1b944a371cd03249e1993c29d899b2679dc17aaceaace1566403aba4e5202d" :answer/id #uuid "ab98b9dc-9aae-5305-b1fa-80643f1ad078" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-4{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333045/selection/d84bf9663907e67ecc7cc6a1a3f9c1c5bb8fd6864cae1a7c6f0a90f235d35b6e" :answer/id #uuid "b1a8d3ed-b266-59a6-b7ec-7c53ccc470a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{19}{4}{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{2}x-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333045/selection/77bdedd18d484ff33a8cc4d1bfaeca7ec6fabb75e43d1ec5d5c658b28cff27ea" :answer/id #uuid "e7d56cee-26d5-5eb4-9240-95543d131e87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{19}{4}{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{11}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333045/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b584940c-7b63-53ec-9eb0-ef15f5f8f1a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-333045/selection/77bdedd18d484ff33a8cc4d1bfaeca7ec6fabb75e43d1ec5d5c658b28cff27ea" :answer-field/choices ["q-333045/selection/a3ea53be90e0ad0edd6de1b4c58b9c898c6d85d84d10a4eda4167c50ef09155c" "q-333045/selection/1f298ed05360d8e0a100b7fe5415ba6d5d8b7db89366bd588d0a3c7df554fba0" "q-333045/selection/3e1b944a371cd03249e1993c29d899b2679dc17aaceaace1566403aba4e5202d" "q-333045/selection/d84bf9663907e67ecc7cc6a1a3f9c1c5bb8fd6864cae1a7c6f0a90f235d35b6e" "q-333045/selection/77bdedd18d484ff33a8cc4d1bfaeca7ec6fabb75e43d1ec5d5c658b28cff27ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333045" :question/id #uuid "19f94b6b-4608-5fc8-9322-fa3e662d1adb" :question/math-academy-id "q-333045" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=4{x}^{2}-3x+2,\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=4{x}^{2}-3x+2\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-3x+2.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +2\\beta )x+(\\beta +2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-3x+2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +2\\beta =-3\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }x) \\\\ \\beta +2\\gamma =2\\,(\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,$ $\\beta =-\\frac{7}{2},$ and $\\gamma =\\frac{11}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial condition.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-2(0)}+2(0)^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}(0)+\\frac{11}{4} & =-2 \\\\ A+\\frac{11}{4} & =-2 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{19}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{19}{4}{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-333045/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333045"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332780/field-1/4ad89910ead110e9f5e80d6ca1525633f4d01c01598dba257d7bafc4edccb38f" :answer/id #uuid "351a888f-ff0d-5b36-9dad-1269415b1d0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2e^{6x}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332780/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "de2350d3-6b2c-559b-a260-10a5fa8d1783" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332780/field-1/4ad89910ead110e9f5e80d6ca1525633f4d01c01598dba257d7bafc4edccb38f" :answer-field/choices ["q-332780/field-1/4ad89910ead110e9f5e80d6ca1525633f4d01c01598dba257d7bafc4edccb38f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332780" :question/id #uuid "23d16a30-c940-5f1b-8608-970838c99c44" :question/math-academy-id "q-332780" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=12,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=12\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a constant, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-6\\beta =12\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\beta $ gives $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6x}-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}-2 & =0 \\\\ A-2 & =0 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\,{e}^{6x}-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332780/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332780"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332988/selection/048a795ac5d013b0a39589a3cd8794e7444748b9b66871b78161caba98f56d43" :answer/id #uuid "22b0f493-effc-55dc-a51b-811605d952c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}-x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-33298